/-
# Click Save again? The 50% limit of a blind retry

My Save button showed the same network error in two runs. One left no draft
on the server. The other left one. Suppose the client can't check which
happened and the server doesn't recognize duplicates. Even if a second POST
is certain to arrive, no retry policy can give better than even odds of
exactly one copy in both cases.

Run `haze submit_receipt.hz` for the whole model. Each block on this page is
editable and runs with the code above it.
-/

/-
## Put the disconnect on either side of the write

I used a [local test form](../Conformance/submit_receipt.py) that makes a
`fetch` request when you click **Save draft**. On the first POST its Python
server closes the connection without sending a response. I moved that cut
from just before the write to just after it. Both times the browser showed
`No receipt (TypeError)`. Its second POST got a normal response.

| First POST disconnected | Rows after first click | Rows after retry |
|---|---:|---:|
| Before saving | 0 | 1 |
| After saving | 1 | 2 |

I counted the rows through `/audit`. That's a view for the person running the
test; the button doesn't consult it. Both requests already carry the same
`Idempotency-Key` header. In these two runs the server ignores it.

Try a coin flip: heads, retry once; tails, stop. In the first Haze block,
`cut` keeps both histories possible without assigning them odds. The only
coin toss is the button's decision. Ask for the chance of exactly one copy:

<!-- run -->

The answer is one half in *each* history. To repeat the browser test rather
than the calculation, save the linked Python file and run
`python submit_receipt.py before`. Open the loopback URL, click Save twice,
and check the count after each click. Restart with `after` for the other run.
-/

enum Cut = BeforeWrite | AfterWrite
param cut : Cut ~= {BeforeWrite: 1, AfterWrite: 1}

def copies(retry : Bool) : Num :=
  (if cut == AfterWrite then 1 else 0) + (if retry then 1 else 0)

def exactly_one(p : Num) : Bool := copies(flip(p)) == 1
#prob exactly_one(0.5)

/-
## Could a different retry rate do better?

Always retry and you duplicate the draft in the second run. Never retry
and you lose it in the first. Suppose you retry with probability `p`.
If the write was lost, retrying is the only way to get one copy: success
has probability `p`. If the write went through, stopping is the only way:
success has probability `1 - p`.

The browser saw the same error, so it must use the same `p` in both cases.
Before you run the block, change `0.9` to `0.8`. Which history gets only a
one-in-five chance of ending up with one copy?

<!-- run -->

It's the case where the draft was already saved. Whatever `p` you pick,
the guarantee across the two cases is `min(p, 1 - p)`. It peaks at `p = 0.5`.
That's where the 50% comes from. It isn't the fraction of real-world
timeouts that leave a saved draft, and it isn't advice to flip a coin
before charging a card. The calculation counts a lost draft and a duplicate
as equally bad; a payment service needn't treat those losses alike.

![For retry chance p, success rises from zero to one if the first write was lost and falls from one to zero if it was saved. The two lines meet at one half.](https://haze-lang.pages.dev/blog/images/submit-retry.svg)
At any retry rate, take the lower of the two lines. Its highest point is where they cross.
-/

#prob exactly_one(0.9)

/-
## Retry the operation, not just the request

Restart the form with `after --keyed`. The first click still shows the
error. This time the second click gets a receipt **and the count stays at
one**. The server now recognizes `draft-1` as the key for this particular
save. A retry reuses it; a different intended save needs its own key.
The fixture checks the key and records it with the row under one lock.
Both vanish when the process exits. A real service needs to commit the key
and its effect atomically and retain them across restarts for the retry window.

Now there are two things to check: **at most one copy**, however many times
we retry, and **at least one copy** by the end of two retries. Give each
retry an independent 90% chance of reaching the server. This is a delivery
rate we choose for the model. The earlier `p` was a decision the client
made. Change `retry_delivery` below to `0.5` to rerun both checks.

<!-- run -->

At 90% delivery, two misses have probability `0.1 * 0.1`. If the first
write was lost, a copy exists after the retries with probability 99%.
If it was saved, a copy already exists. At 50% delivery the lower number
falls to 75%. Neither rate produces a second copy. Those are results about
the stored row, not promises that the browser gets a receipt.

[HTTP's retry rule](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-9.2.2)
allows for knowing that an operation is idempotent or that the original
request was never applied. An empty search result while the first write
is still in flight establishes neither. For a [browser agent](https://browyhq.github.io/blog/how-i-built-browy/)
on someone else's site, permission to click isn't evidence that the first
click failed. Unless the site can settle that question or honor the same
key, the honest status to show the user is **save unconfirmed**.
-/

system Save(dedup : Bool, delivery : Num) {
  var seen_key : Bool := cut == AfterWrite
  var rows : Num := if cut == AfterWrite then 1 else 0
  step {
    if flip(delivery) {
      if not (dedup and seen_key) { rows := rows + 1 }
      seen_key := true
    }
  }
}

def retry_delivery : Num := 0.9
theorem no_duplicate : Save(true, retry_delivery) |= always rows <= 1
#prob Save(true, retry_delivery) |= after[2] rows >= 1
